<body>
<div class="Section1">

<!-- COVER BANNER -->
<div class="cover-box">
    <div class="cover-title">ALBERTO WATCH COMPANY</div>
    <div class="cover-subtitle">Complete Technical & Architectural Documentation</div>
    <div class="cover-meta">
        <strong>Established:</strong> 1987 &nbsp;|&nbsp;
        <strong>Framework:</strong> React 19 &nbsp;|&nbsp;
        <strong>Build System:</strong> Vite 8 &nbsp;|&nbsp;
        <strong>Iconography:</strong> Lucide React & Custom SVG &nbsp;|&nbsp;
        <strong>Date:</strong> October 2026
    </div>
</div>

<!-- EXECUTIVE SUMMARY -->
<h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>
<p>
    <strong>Alberto Watch Company</strong> is a premier luxury watch retailer and certified horological restoration service. 
    Founded in 1987 by Swiss-trained master watchmaker Alberto Reyes, the brand operates retail flagships in New York, 
    Beverly Hills, and Chicago, holding authorized dealer status for Rolex, Michael Kors, Citizen Eco-Drive, and Bulova.
</p>
<p>
    The web application is an ultra-responsive, accessible single-page application built on <strong>React 19</strong> and <strong>Vite 8</strong>. 
    It features a bespoke luxury dark-gold design system, interactive product catalogs with modal specifications, live boutique locator, 
    fullscreen lightbox gallery, and an infinite real-time status ticker with geolocation and live ticking clock.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <div class="callout-title">Key Architectural Highlights</div>
    All legacy emojis and plain unicode bullets have been cleanly transitioned to high-precision <strong>Lucide React</strong> icons 
    and custom SVG components for uniform luxury branding, cross-browser consistency, and full screen-reader accessibility.
</div>

<hr/>

<!-- QUICK START -->
<h2>2. Getting Started & Troubleshooting</h2>

<div class="callout-info">
    <div class="callout-title">Directory Navigation Notice</div>
    The web application repository resides inside the subfolder <code>alberto-watch-company</code>. Running <code>npm</code> commands 
    from the parent <code>Moses</code> workspace directory will return an <code>ENOENT (package.json not found)</code> error.
</div>

<h3>Standard Launch Commands</h3>
<pre><code># Step 1: Navigate into the application directory
cd alberto-watch-company

# Step 2: Install dependencies (if not already installed)
npm install

# Step 3: Start the local development server with Hot Module Reloading (HMR)
npm run dev</code></pre>

<h3>Alternative: Running from Workspace Root</h3>
<p>To run directly from the parent workspace without changing directories, use the <code>--prefix</code> flag:</p>
<pre><code>npm --prefix alberto-watch-company run dev</code></pre>

<h3>Core NPM Scripts</h3>
<table>
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Command</th>
            <th>Function</th>
            <th>Target Environment</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><code>npm run dev</code></td>
            <td>Starts local Vite dev server with instant HMR</td>
            <td>Development (<code>http://localhost:5173/</code>)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>npm run build</code></td>
            <td>Compiles, tree-shakes, and minifies assets into <code>dist/</code></td>
            <td>Production Build</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>npm run preview</code></td>
            <td>Locally serves the compiled <code>dist/</code> production build</td>
            <td>Pre-deployment Testing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>npm run lint</code></td>
            <td>Runs Oxlint AST analysis for code quality & react patterns</td>
            <td>Code Quality</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<hr/>

<!-- TECHNOLOGY STACK -->
<h2>3. Technology Stack</h2>
<table>
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Layer</th>
            <th>Package</th>
            <th>Version</th>
            <th>Description & Responsibility</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>UI Framework</strong></td>
            <td><code>react</code>, <code>react-dom</code></td>
            <td><code>^19.3.1</code></td>
            <td>Component rendering, virtual DOM reconciliation, state hooks.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Build Engine</strong></td>
            <td><code>vite</code>, <code>@vitejs/plugin-react</code></td>
            <td><code>^8.3.5</code></td>
            <td>Ultra-fast development server, asset pipeline, Rollup production bundling.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Iconography</strong></td>
            <td><code>lucide-react</code></td>
            <td><code>^1.63.0</code></td>
            <td>Standardized 24x24 stroke vector iconography for UI controls and specifications.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Branded Icons</strong></td>
            <td><code>SocialIcons.jsx</code></td>
            <td>Custom SVG</td>
            <td>Custom SVG components for Instagram, Facebook, X (Twitter), and YouTube.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Linter</strong></td>
            <td><code>oxlint</code></td>
            <td><code>^1.89.0</code></td>
            <td>Rust-based high-speed linter verifying syntax, imports, and hooks purity.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Styling</strong></td>
            <td>Vanilla CSS3 (<code>index.css</code>)</td>
            <td>CSS Variables</td>
            <td>Zero-dependency luxury dark design system, CSS Grid/Flexbox, hardware-accelerated animations.</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

